<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Trench</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/627">627</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Khandaq, Battle of the Confederates</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Trench was fought in 627, when a confederate force estimated at around 10,000 men, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> with the exiled Banu Nadir and several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes, advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. At the suggestion of Salman the Persian, Muhammad's followers dug a trench around the settlement, which held the attackers back for about two weeks until bad weather and quarrels within the confederacy led them to withdraw. Reported casualties were few. The Muslims then besieged the Banu Qurayza, who surrendered, and a judgment pronounced by Sa'd ibn Mu'adh was carried out; scholars doubt Ibn Ishaq's account of the numbers killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trench.jpg" alt="Battle of the Trench" width="330" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DrZubairRashid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Trench_627_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After their expulsion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, the Muslims fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-badr">Battle of Badr</a> in 624 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-uhud">Battle of Uhud</a> in 625, where they were defeated. Early in 627 leaders of the exiled Banu Nadir, among them Huyayy ibn Akhtab, travelled from Khaybar to Mecca to forge an alliance aimed at eliminating the Muslim community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. They enlisted the Banu Ghatafan by paying them half of their harvest, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-asad">Banu Asad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-sulaym">Banu Sulaym</a> joined, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-amir">Banu Amir</a>, who had a pact with Muhammad, refused. Scholars do not agree on numbers, but the Confederates are estimated at around 10,000 men and six hundred horsemen, led by Abu Sufyan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Medinans considered meeting the enemy in the open or waiting inside the city, and chose to dig a trench, a tactic introduced by Salman the Persian. Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> already had fortress-like houses and rocky ground, the trench was dug only in certain places, with the longest stretch on the northern front. Every capable Muslim, Muhammad included, helped dig it in six days, during a near-famine. The defenders numbered 3,000 and included all Medinans over fourteen except the Banu Qurayza, who supplied some digging tools.</p><p>Since sieges were uncommon in Arabian warfare, the Confederates were unprepared for the trench. Their horsemen could not force a crossing, and the two sides exchanged insults and arrows from a distance. Huyayy persuaded the Banu Qurayza to renounce their pact with Muhammad, and rumours of an attack from their quarter demoralised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. Nu'aym ibn Mas'ud, a secret convert, sowed distrust between the Confederates and the Qurayza. Supplies ran short, the weather turned cold and wet, and the Confederates withdrew during the night.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Muslims then besieged the Banu Qurayza, who surrendered unconditionally after 25 days. At the request of the Banu Aws, their allies, Muhammad named Sa'd ibn Mu'adh as arbitrator, and he decreed that some of the men fighters be killed and some women and children enslaved. According to Ibn Ishaq the men numbered between 400 and 900, but many scholars doubt his account, and no reliable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> gives the number, which leads some to argue that only prominent men were executed.</p><p>The failed siege cost the Meccans their trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and much of their prestige, and Watt conjectures that they began to think conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the most prudent course.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Trench” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
